@end menu

@node Graphcore Firewall
@section The firewall

Graphcore is in the @strong{third translation-unit class}: it includes
neither @file{lisp.h} nor @file{<libregnum.h>}, only @file{<glib.h>}.

This is the most important property of the subsystem.  raylib's
@code{Color} is a struct and cmacs's @file{pgtkgui.h} @code{Color} is a
@code{void *}; they cannot coexist in one translation unit, which is
what forces every libregnum subsystem into a render half, a Lisp half,
and a pure model.  Graphcore is entirely that third class.

The payoff is testability.  Because the solver links against nothing
but glib, all of it runs with no Lisp VM, no GL context and no display:

@example
just test-graphcore          # or: make -C cmacs/graphcore/tests check
@end example

@file{cmacs/graphcore/tests/config.h} is a two-line shim standing in
for Emacs's gnulib-laden @file{src/config.h}.  If it ever needs to
grow, something has breached the firewall.

@node Graphcore Data Model
@section Data model

Nodes are keyed by a caller-chosen @strong{id string}, never by index.
Indices are dense and stable only within one finalized generation; the
id is what survives a rebuild, so it is also what is handed to the
libregnum node table as the pick @code{path}.

Besides the obvious fields (@code{title}, @code{file}, @code{group},
@code{level}, @code{pos}, @code{rgba}, degrees and the CSR slice), a
node carries @code{x, y, z} for its position, @code{vx, vy, vz} as
solver scratch, @code{sx@dots{}sz} and @code{tx@dots{}tz} as tween
endpoints, and @code{parent}, @code{descendants} and @code{ring} for
hierarchy and band assignment.

Tween endpoints are deliberately @emph{not} aliased onto @code{x/y/z}
or the solver's scratch.  A tween and a solve are different
animations, and overlapping their storage makes an interrupted
transition land somewhere neither intended.

An edge is @code{(a, b, kind, weight)}, where @code{kind} is @code{id}
(the real topology), @code{cite}, or @code{sim} (the ai-brigade
semantic index --- an overlay, weighted 0.35 so it nudges without
competing with real structure, and excluded from link navigation).
@code{add_edge} drops self-loops, dangling endpoints, and duplicates on
the @emph{unordered} pair plus kind, so @code{A->B} and @code{B->A}
collapse into one spring.

@code{cmacs_graph_add_node} returns a @strong{signed} index:

@example
gint idx = cmacs_graph_add_node (g, id, title, file, group,
                                 level, pos, rgba);
if (idx < 0) continue;   /* graph is full -- drop it */
@end example

Returning @code{0} for ``full'' aliased every overflowing node onto
node 0, silently corrupting the graph rather than dropping the node.

@node Graphcore Force Layout
@section The force layout

Fruchterman--Reingold with Barnes--Hut repulsion.  Stepped, not
one-shot: @code{begin} seeds and arms it, @code{step} advances a few
iterations and reports convergence, and the Lisp side drives it from a
timer so the graph visibly unfolds and then stops.

Choices worth knowing:

@itemize @bullet
@item
@code{k} (ideal edge length) is a @emph{constant}, not
@code{sqrt(area/n)}.  That formula exists to pack a graph into a fixed
frame; here the camera fits itself to the content, so making @code{k}
depend on @code{n} would only make the map's scale depend on how many
notes you happen to have.
@item
Gravity is proportional to distance @emph{and} to
@code{sqrt(degree)}.  Weighting by degree is what makes it look like a
knowledge graph rather than a soap bubble.
@item
Displacement is damped by @code{log2(1 + degree)}.  Without it the
high-degree hubs whip around and drag the whole map.
@item
Convergence requires three consecutive quiet iterations.  One is not
enough: the first iterations of a cold-started component can be
near-still by accident.
@item
Setting @code{theta} to 0 degenerates Barnes--Hut to an exact
all-pairs sum, which the tests use as a free reference implementation.
@end itemize

@node Graphcore Placement
@section Closed-form placement

@code{CIRCLE}, @code{HEX} and @code{RINGS} compute a final position for
every node in a single pass.  There is nothing to step and no
convergence --- running the solver over them would only undo them,
which is why @code{place} marks the layout converged.

@example
cmacs_graph_layout_place (l, g, CMACS_GRAPH_LAYOUT_RINGS, 2);
cmacs_graph_layout_tween_begin (l, g, 24);   /* or _snap (g) */
@end example

@table @code
@item CMACS_GRAPH_LAYOUT_CIRCLE
One concentric circle per distinct @code{group}, in first-appearance
order.
@item CMACS_GRAPH_LAYOUT_HEX
Axial hex lattice spiralling out from the origin.  Ring @var{r} holds
6@var{r} cells; the traversal starts at the ring's @code{DIR[2]} corner
and walks from @code{DIR[4]}.  Starting from @code{DIR[0]} instead
re-visits cells and lands two nodes on the same point --- the lattice
test catches exactly that.
@item CMACS_GRAPH_LAYOUT_RINGS
Concentric bands taken from @code{node->ring}, members spread by angle,
groups kept contiguous so a department reads as one wedge.  Alternate
bands counter-rotate under spin so the motion reads as depth.  This is
the ARMS layout.
@end table

@subsection RINGS: departments get proportional wedges

@code{RINGS} does not spread band members evenly.  Each @emph{group
root} --- a visible node with no visible ancestor, that is, a department
hub --- is given a contiguous angular @strong{wedge} sized by how many
visible nodes it holds, and its members are laid out inside that wedge
on a wide, shallow lattice sitting just outside the band radius.

Three decisions, each with a failure it exists to prevent:

@itemize @bullet
@item
@strong{Wedges are proportional, not equal.}  Equal wedges give a
department of four notes the same arc as one of four thousand, so the
big group piles onto itself while the small one floats in empty space.
That is what turns ``show me everything'' into an unreadable smear.
@item
@strong{The group root is the outermost visible ancestor}, not the
immediate parent.  A grandchild belongs to its @emph{department}, so
opening a subfolder does not eject its contents from the wedge.
@item
@strong{Members sit just outside their hub}, not straddling it.  The
group then reads as hanging off its department, and an expand animates
outward --- the direction the gesture implies.
@end itemize

A collapsed parent's children are invisible and so are not placed at
all; the next expand therefore animates them out of the hub's own
position rather than out of wherever a stale placement had scattered
them.

@subsection CIRCLE: children fan around their parent

@code{CIRCLE} has no bands to budget, so it uses a simpler second pass:
a node whose parent is visible is skipped by the ring pass and fanned
out of that parent on a phyllotaxis spiral (golden angle, radius growing
as the square root of rank so density stays even), offset outward along
the direction the parent already sits in.

@code{RINGS} deliberately does @emph{not} use this pass.  A fan sized
independently of the wedge it sits in overflows into the neighbouring
department the moment a big group opens.  @code{HEX} is a lattice by
definition and is left alone entirely.

@strong{Placement writes to @code{tx/ty/tz} and moves nothing.}
Separating ``decide where things go'' from ``move them there'' is what
lets one code path serve both an instant switch and an animated one,
and it keeps the layouts testable without running an animation.

Only @emph{visible} nodes are placed: a collapsed subtree has no
position to occupy, and including it would leave gaps in every ring.

Determinism is a requirement, not a nicety.  These are re-run on every
spin tick and every expand, so ordering is always by an explicit key
(ring, then group name, then index) and never by hash iteration order.

@node Graphcore Tweening
@section Tweening

@example
cmacs_graph_layout_tween_begin (l, g, frames);
while (!cmacs_graph_layout_tween_step (l, g)) @{ /* one per frame */ @}
@end example

Ease-in-out cubic.  A linear tween starts and stops abruptly, which
reads as a jump-cut rather than as motion.

@itemize @bullet
@item
@code{frames <= 0} snaps.
@item
The final step lands @emph{exactly} on the target rather than merely
near it.  Accumulated float error would otherwise leave the graph a
hair off its own layout, which shows up as drift after repeated
switches.
@item
@code{tween_begin} mid-flight re-snapshots from wherever the nodes
currently @emph{are}, so an interrupted transition continues smoothly
instead of rewinding to its origin.
@end itemize

Placing @code{FORCE} aims every node at its current position, so a
caller that tweens unconditionally after a layout switch does not drag
the graph to the origin.

@node Graphcore Hierarchy
@section Hierarchy and collapse

Optional.  A graph that never calls @code{set_parent} behaves exactly
as it did before this existed: every node a visible root, no walks, no
cost.

@example
cmacs_graph_set_parent    (g, child_idx, parent_idx);  /* pre-finalize */
cmacs_graph_set_collapsed (g, idx, TRUE);
cmacs_graph_collapse_all  (g, TRUE);
guint shown = cmacs_graph_n_visible (g);
@end example

@code{finalize} computes @code{descendants} (the transitive child
count, used to size a collapsed hub) and initial visibility.  A node is
visible unless some ancestor is collapsed.

Descendant counting is @emph{bottom-up over a depth-sorted order}, not
a recursive walk: recursion on a pathological tree --- 35k files nested
one per level --- would overflow the C stack, and it is O(n) either
way.

@code{set_parent} @strong{refuses} a cycle rather than propagating it.
Every consumer of the field walks upward, so a cycle is a hang, not a
wrong answer.  It is cheap to check at the point of creation and
impossible to diagnose later.

@node Graphcore Consumers
@section What a consumer does

A consumer supplies data and owns rendering:

@itemize @bullet
@item
Build with @code{begin_update} / @code{add_node} / @code{add_edge} /
optionally @code{set_parent} / @code{finalize}.
@item
Drive the solver with @code{layout_begin} / @code{layout_step}, or
place a closed-form layout with @code{layout_place} and animate with
the tween.
@item
Read @code{x/y/z}, @code{radius}, @code{rgba}, @code{visible} and
@code{descendants} when building the scene.
@end itemize

Graphcore never allocates a drawable, never touches a Lisp object and
never talks to GL.  Everything it produces is numbers on a struct.

@subsection Emission order is not graph order

A consumer that skips nodes --- invisible ones, or ones past a drawable
budget --- @strong{must} keep a graph-index to emission-index map.  The
scene's node table and shape arrays are in emission order while the
graph is in graph order, and the two diverge the moment anything is
skipped.  Reading node flags with a graph index then recolours the
wrong nodes.  See @code{node_emit} / @code{edge_emit} in
@file{cmacs-roamgraph-scene.c} for the worked example.

@node Graphcore Limits
@section What you cannot extend from here

@itemize @bullet
@item
@strong{Node geometry.}  Graphcore has no opinion about shapes; that is
the consumer's scene builder.  roamgraph draws one sphere per note by
policy, not by limitation.
@item
@strong{Edge routing.}  Straight lines only, at every consumer so far.
Nothing here forbids curves, but nothing provides them either.
@item
@strong{Per-node layout overrides.}  Pinning is the escape hatch: a
pinned node is not moved by the solver, so precomputing positions and
pinning everything is how you get a layout that does not exist here.
@item
@strong{Incremental layout.}  @code{begin} re-seeds.  Survivors keep
their positions, so a rebuild does not teleport, but there is no ``add
one node and relax only its neighbourhood'' entry point ---
@code{reheat} is the closest thing.
@end itemize

@node Graphcore Tests
@section Tests

@file{cmacs/graphcore/tests/test-graphcore.c}, 25 GTest cases, all
headless.

@table @code
@item /graphcore/graph/
Lookup, duplicate ids, @emph{overflow returns -1}, edge sanitising,
position inheritance across a rebuild.
@item /graphcore/hierarchy/
Absent by default, descendant counts, collapse hides a subtree,
transitivity, collapse-all, @emph{cycle refusal}.
@item /graphcore/layout/
Convergence, 2D planarity, determinism.
@item /graphcore/place/
Circle radii per group, hex is a real lattice, ring bands ordered
outward, invisible nodes skipped, determinism, spin rotates without
reshaping.
@item /graphcore/tween/
Lands exactly, monotonic, zero frames snaps, restart continues from
current, @code{FORCE} holds still.
@end table

The hex and overflow tests are both regressions with real bite: the
first caught a ring traversal that placed two nodes on the same point,
the second guards the aliasing bug described in
@ref{Graphcore Data Model}.
